  "name": "C5 COMPLEMENT",
  "code": "1049",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"111\" data-end=\"314\"><strong data-start=\"111\" data-end=\"142\">C5 (Complement Component 5)</strong> is a critical part of the <strong data-start=\"169\" data-end=\"200\">terminal complement pathway</strong>, which contributes to the formation of the <strong data-start=\"244\" data-end=\"277\">membrane attack complex (MAC)</strong>, essential for destroying pathogens.</p>\n<p data-start=\"316\" data-end=\"337\">This test is used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"339\" data-end=\"790\">\n<li data-start=\"339\" data-end=\"474\">\n<p data-start=\"341\" data-end=\"474\">Evaluate <strong data-start=\"350\" data-end=\"377\">complement deficiencies</strong>, especially in patients with <strong data-start=\"407\" data-end=\"441\">recurrent bacterial infections</strong> (e.g., <em data-start=\"449\" data-end=\"473\">Neisseria meningitidis</em>)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"475\" data-end=\"571\">\n<p data-start=\"477\" data-end=\"571\">Investigate <strong data-start=\"489\" data-end=\"517\">immune complex disorders</strong> and <strong data-start=\"522\" data-end=\"545\">autoimmune diseases</strong> (e.g., lupus, vasculitis)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"572\" data-end=\"706\">\n<p data-start=\"574\" data-end=\"706\">Monitor patients receiving <strong data-start=\"601\" data-end=\"627\">C5-inhibitor therapies</strong> (e.g., <strong data-start=\"635\" data-end=\"649\">eculizumab</strong> for paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria or atypical HUS)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"707\" data-end=\"790\">\n<p data-start=\"709\" data-end=\"790\">Study <strong data-start=\"715\" data-end=\"747\">complement-mediated diseases</strong>, such as <strong data-start=\"757\" data-end=\"765\">aHUS</strong> or <strong data-start=\"769\" data-end=\"790\">C3 glomerulopathy</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong data-start=\"769\" data-end=\"790\">Decreased C5 &ndash; Clinical Implications</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>C5 Deficiency (Inherited)</strong></td>\n<td>Leads to increased risk of severe, recurrent <strong>Neisseria infections</strong> (e.g., meningitis)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE)</strong></td>\n<td>May show decreased C5 due to immune complex-driven complement consumption</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Atypical Hemolytic Uremic Syndrome (aHUS)</strong></td>\n<td>Dysregulation of the terminal complement pathway may involve C5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Eculizumab therapy monitoring</strong></td>\n<td>C5 levels may be assessed to determine the impact of <strong>C5-blocking drugs</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In patients with recurrent meningococcal infections, testing C5&ndash;C9 (terminal complement components) is often indicated.</p>",
